<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tokugawa Yoshinobu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">resigned <a href="/years/1867">1867</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tokugawa Yoshinobu was the fifteenth and last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogun</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate</a>. Facing mounting pressure from anti-shogunate domains and the challenges posed by Western powers, he resigned his powers to the emperor in 1867. Accounts of the Tokugawa Shogunate treat his resignation as the end of shogunal rule, which was followed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tokugawa-yoshinobu.jpg" alt="Tokugawa Yoshinobu" width="259" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Frederick Sutton · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1867_Osaka_Yoshinobu_Tokugawa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under mounting pressure, the last shogun, <b>Tokugawa Yoshinobu</b>, returned his governing authority to the young Emperor Meiji in November 1867, and the restoration of imperial rule was proclaimed on 3 January 1868.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>On 8 November 1867 <b>Tokugawa Yoshinobu</b> returned power to the Imperial Court, and on 3 January 1868 domain troops seized the Imperial Palace as the Emperor declared his authority restored.</blockquote><a href="/movement/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>Nariaki's son became <b>Tokugawa Yoshinobu</b>, the last shogun.</blockquote><a href="/state/mito-domain">Mito Domain</a></li><li><blockquote>The last lord, Ikeda Yoshinori, a brother of the last shogun <b>Tokugawa Yoshinobu</b>, nevertheless sided with the imperial forces in 1868.</blockquote><a href="/state/tottori-domain">Tottori Domain</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Koishikawa, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. Died in Koishikawa-ku, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.71,139.75&amp;year=1867" aria-label="Koishikawa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8078 874 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Tokugawa Yoshinobu"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8528 1144h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8528 1144h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.71,139.75&amp;year=1867">What was here in 1867? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
